pa-models
=========

Origin
------
This crate is a modified fork of the `pa-models` crate from
PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent (https://github.com/PrimeIntellect-ai/prime-agent),
the Rust side of the Prime Agent product.

Upstream in turn is a Rust port of the product's TypeScript catalog client: the
files `model-catalog-cache.ts`, `model-catalog.ts`, `model-compat-schema.ts`,
`provider-model-catalog.ts`, `bundled-model-catalog.ts`, and
`prime-inference-model-catalog.ts` (upstream branch `feat/catalog-client`). The
Rust module layout follows them one-to-one:

  src/chain.rs            <- model-catalog.ts            (no-cold-start chain, refresh cadence)
  src/cache.rs            <- model-catalog-cache.ts      (scope-keyed last-good cache)
  src/compat.rs           <- model-compat-schema.ts      (per-`api` compat validation)
  src/pinning.rs          <- provider-model-catalog.ts   (transport pinning)
  src/bundled.rs          <- bundled-model-catalog.ts    (packaged snapshot assets)
  src/prime_inference.rs  <- prime-inference-model-catalog.ts

Upstream license: MIT. The upstream license text is vendored at
`vendor/LICENSE-prime-agent`; it also credits the original TypeScript product,
"a port of the Prime Agent TypeScript product, originally Copyright (c) 2025
Mario Zechner". That MIT license and copyright notice apply to this crate.

Modifications by oh-my-tau
--------------------------
This copy has been changed. It is NOT byte-identical to upstream, and files
carrying the upstream header are not verbatim upstream content. Substantive
changes:

* Port, not a copy: the crate is a Rust rewrite of the TypeScript catalog
  client rather than a translation of an upstream Rust source file, so module
  names, types, and function signatures are this repo's own. Only the wire
  contracts (`models/catalog.v1.json`, `plugins/catalog.v2.json`, and the Prime
  Inference response shapes) are held to the TS client's behavior.
* HTTP client: `reqwest` with `default-features = false` and only `rustls-tls`,
  replacing whatever TLS/transport stack upstream used; the fetch layer enforces
  a 5 s timeout, an 8 MiB cap (content-length and streaming), refused redirects,
  and ETag `If-None-Match` / 304 snapshot reuse.
* Cache scoping added for this repo: `CatalogCache` is keyed by source scope and
  Prime Inference scope keys are HMAC-SHA256 of the credential over the team id,
  which is why `hmac`, `sha2`, and `hex` appear in Cargo.toml. A `401`/`403`
  clears only its own scope.
* `PI_OFFLINE` handling (src/offline.rs) and the compiled fallback table in
  src/transports.rs are this repo's: the fallback universe is fixed at build
  time to the transports this client compiles in, and pinning refuses any
  catalog model that does not match a compiled `(provider, api, baseUrl)` tuple.
  Catalog data never carries request headers.
* Compat validation (src/compat.rs) is a structural re-implementation of the TS
  validators, deliberately independent of the permissive `pa_types` wire structs,
  and the accepted key/type sets are adjusted to the models this repo targets.
* Strict deserialization is enforced throughout (`deny_unknown_fields`,
  `schemaVersion == 1`, an entry carrying a `headers` key is rejected), so a
  future catalog version keeps the last-good snapshot instead of erroring.
* Crate metadata is inherited from the workspace (`version`, `edition`,
  `license`, `[lints]`), and dependencies on `pa-ai` and `pa-types` are local
  path deps on this repo's forks rather than crates.io versions.

